<p class="lead">Good treestand bowhunting tips come down to two things: making a clean shot from an unusual angle, and getting home safely every time. Shooting from a treestand changes your shot angle, your form, your ranging and the way a deer&rsquo;s vitals line up with your pin. None of it is complicated, but all of it has to be practiced before the season, not figured out when a buck is under your stand.</p>

<p>This Better Outdoors guide covers where to aim from elevation, how to hold form when shooting down, how to use angle-compensated ranging, the safety habits that matter most, how to choose stand height and placement for the wind, and how to practice so the real shot feels familiar.</p>

<?= tool_cta('bowhunting-checklist') ?>

<h2 id="h-why-shooting-from-a-treestand-is-different">Why Shooting From a Treestand Is Different</h2>
<p>On the ground, most shots are close to level. You see the deer broadside, aim behind the shoulder and the arrow travels through the chest on a flat path. From a treestand, the arrow enters high and exits low. That one change affects everything:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The vitals shrink.</strong> Seen from above, the lungs present a narrower target, and the spine sits directly in the arrow&rsquo;s path if you aim too high.</li>
  <li><strong>The exit moves.</strong> A steep arrow can enter in the right place and still exit too high to pass through both lungs.</li>
  <li><strong>Distance gets confusing.</strong> The line-of-sight distance from your stand to the deer is longer than the horizontal distance the arrow actually has to &ldquo;fight&rdquo; gravity over.</li>
  <li><strong>Form gets compromised.</strong> Seated, twisted or reaching around the tree, it is easy to drop your bow arm and lose your anchor.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every tip below targets one of these problems.</p>

<h2 id="h-bow-shot-angle-from-treestand-aim-for-the-exit">Bow Shot Angle From a Treestand: Aim for the Exit</h2>
<p>The most useful mental shift for treestand shot placement is to stop thinking about where the arrow goes in and start thinking about where it comes out. Picture the line from your stand through the deer. Your goal is an arrow path that passes through <strong>both lungs</strong> (and ideally the top of the heart) and exits low on the far side.</p>
<p>In practice, that usually means your aim point sits a little lower on the near side than it would from the ground. The closer and steeper the shot, the more this matters. A deer standing almost directly below you leaves very little lung to work with, and the shoulder blade and spine guard much of what is left.</p>
<h3 id="h-the-quartering-away-shot">The Quartering-Away Shot</h3>
<p>For most stand hunters, the <strong>quartering away shot</strong> is the best opportunity you will get. When the deer&rsquo;s near-side front leg is stepped forward and its body angles away from you, the arrow can slip in behind the last ribs and angle forward through both lungs. From elevation, aim so the arrow would exit around the off-side front leg. That gives you plenty of margin for error in every direction.</p>
