<h2 id="h-bend-at-the-waist-form-from-elevation">Bend at the Waist: Shooting Form From Elevation</h2>
<p>The single biggest form mistake in a stand is shooting down by dropping the bow arm. When you do that, your anchor slides, your head position changes, and your peep, sight and eye no longer line up the way they do on the target range. Arrows usually go high.</p>
<p>Instead, <strong>bend at the waist</strong>. Draw the bow with your arms in their normal shooting shape, as if the target were level, then hinge at the hips until the pin settles on the deer. Your upper body stays a rigid &ldquo;T,&rdquo; so your anchor, peep alignment and bow-arm angle stay exactly the same as they are on the practice range.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Stand when you can.</strong> Standing makes it easier to bend at the waist and turn your whole body. If you hunt seated, practice seated.</li>
  <li><strong>Turn your feet, not just your shoulders.</strong> Set your feet so you can rotate toward the likely shooting lanes without twisting your spine.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch for clearance.</strong> Check that your lower limb and cams won&rsquo;t hit the platform, your knee or the tree when you bend.</li>
  <li><strong>Level the bubble.</strong> Canting is more common at awkward angles. A quick glance at your sight level helps.</li>
</ul>
<p>Shorter, compact bows are easier to maneuver around a trunk and in a cramped stand. Bows such as the one in our <?= post_link('2018-pse-evolve-28', 'PSE Evolve 28 review') ?> or the ready-to-go setup covered in our <?= post_link('bear-archery-whitetail-maxx-rth-compound-bow', 'Bear Whitetail Maxx RTH review') ?> are good examples of the kind of manageable package that works well from a stand.</p>

<h2 id="h-rangefinder-angle-compensation">Rangefinder Angle Compensation and Ranging</h2>
<p>Gravity acts on an arrow over the <strong>horizontal</strong> distance to the target, not the straight-line distance. From a treestand, the straight-line distance to a deer is always a bit longer than the horizontal one. At normal stand heights and typical bow ranges the difference is often small, but on close, steep shots it can be enough to push your arrow high if you hold for the line-of-sight number.</p>
<p>That is why <strong>rangefinder angle compensation</strong> is so useful. A rangefinder with an angle or &ldquo;bow mode&rdquo; reports the horizontal (true ballistic) distance, so you can choose a pin the same way you would on flat ground. If you shop for one, look for an angle-compensating model such as a <?= amz('angle compensating rangefinder bow hunting', 'bowhunting rangefinder with angle compensation') ?>.</p>
<p>Ranging habits that help:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Range landmarks as soon as you settle in.</strong> A rock, a stump, a fence post, the edge of a trail. Knowing those distances means you don&rsquo;t need to move when a deer shows up.</li>
  <li><strong>Range at deer height.</strong> Aim the rangefinder at the ground or a point at chest height, not at a branch above the trail.</li>
  <li><strong>Make a mental (or written) range card.</strong> Some hunters draw a quick sketch of their shooting lanes with yardages.</li>
  <li><strong>Re-range if the deer is between markers.</strong> Only if you can do it without being seen.</li>
</ol>
