<h2 id="h-treestand-safety-harness-and-lifelines">Treestand Safety: Harness, Lifeline and Three Points of Contact</h2>
<p>No treestand bowhunting tip matters more than this section. Falls happen most often while climbing up, climbing down, or moving between the ladder or steps and the platform. The Treestand Manufacturers Association (TMA) and state hunter-education programs all push the same core habits, and they are worth following every single time.</p>
<h3 id="h-wear-a-full-body-harness">Wear a Full-Body Harness</h3>
<p>Use a <strong>treestand safety harness</strong> that meets current industry standards (look for TMA-certified gear), and wear it correctly from the time you leave the ground. A full-body harness spreads the load across your chest, waist and legs if you fall. Single belts or chest-only straps are not substitutes. Read the manufacturer&rsquo;s instructions, check the expiration or replacement guidance printed on it, and practice wearing it until putting it on is automatic.</p>
<h3 id="h-stay-connected-with-a-lifeline">Stay Connected With a Lifeline</h3>
<p>A harness only protects you when it is attached to the tree. A <strong>lifeline</strong> (a rope with a sliding prusik knot, anchored above the stand and at the base of the tree) keeps you connected while you climb up and down. Once you are on the platform, attach your tree strap above head height so there is minimal slack. Many hunters leave a lifeline installed on each stand for the season.</p>
<h3 id="h-three-points-of-contact">Three Points of Contact</h3>
<p>When climbing, keep three points of contact (two hands and a foot, or two feet and a hand) at all times. Climb slowly, especially in rain, frost or darkness when steps are slick.</p>
<h3 id="h-use-a-bow-hoist-and-never-climb-with-your-bow">Use a Bow Hoist and Never Climb With Your Bow</h3>
<p>Never climb with your bow, a loaded crossbow, or your pack in your hands. Tie a <strong>bow hoist</strong> rope to your stand, climb up, get attached, then pull up your bow. Always hoist it <strong>unloaded</strong>, with arrows secured in a covered quiver so broadheads are guarded.</p>
