<h2 id="h-best-treestand-height-for-bowhunting">Best Treestand Height for Bowhunting</h2>
<p>There is no single best treestand height for bowhunting. Many bowhunters hang stands somewhere around 15 to 20 feet, but the right height for your spot depends on cover, terrain and how far you expect to shoot. Higher is not automatically better: every extra foot makes the shot angle steeper at close range and adds risk on the climb.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Use cover over height.</strong> A stand tucked into a trunk with branches behind you hides your outline better than a bare tree hung extra high.</li>
  <li><strong>Mind the terrain.</strong> On a hillside, a stand on the uphill side of a trail sits effectively lower relative to deer; on the downhill side, it sits higher.</li>
  <li><strong>Think about shot distance.</strong> If most shots will be under 15 yards, a lower stand keeps the angle less steep and the vitals more exposed.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep it practical.</strong> Choose a height you can climb safely and comfortably in the dark.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-stand-placement-and-wind">Stand Placement and Wind</h2>
<p>A perfect shot is impossible if deer smell you first. Plan every stand around wind direction:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hang stands downwind</strong> of where you expect deer to travel or feed, based on the prevailing wind for that location.</li>
  <li><strong>Have options.</strong> Set up two or three stands for different winds and only hunt each one when the wind is right.</li>
  <li><strong>Plan your entry and exit.</strong> Walking through a bedding area or across a feeding field to reach your stand can ruin a spot for days.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch thermals.</strong> Air tends to drift downhill in the cool of morning and evening and uphill as the day warms.</li>
  <li><strong>Clear shooting lanes carefully</strong> (and only where allowed). Trim just enough for a clean arrow path to your likely shot spots.</li>
</ul>
<p>Finally, set your stand so your most likely shot is to your <strong>non-dominant side</strong> or straight in front of you. For a right-handed archer, that usually means deer passing on the left, where you can draw and bend at the waist without twisting around the trunk.</p>

<h2 id="h-practice-from-elevation">Practice From Elevation Before the Season</h2>
<p>You would not hunt with a bow you had never sighted in, so don&rsquo;t hunt from a stand you have never shot from. Practice from elevation in the weeks before your season:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Set up a safe practice stand.</strong> A ladder stand or platform in your yard or at a club works, with your harness on and attached, exactly as you would hunt.</li>
  <li><strong>Use a 3D deer target.</strong> A <?= amz('3d deer archery target', '3D deer target') ?> shows you how the vitals look from above and makes &ldquo;aim for the exit&rdquo; real.</li>
  <li><strong>Shoot several angles and distances.</strong> Broadside, quartering away, close and far. Note where arrows exit.</li>
  <li><strong>Wear your hunting clothes.</strong> Bulky jackets and a harness change how the string clears your chest and arm.</li>
  <li><strong>Practice seated and standing.</strong> Whatever you might do in the stand, do it in practice.</li>
  <li><strong>Make the first shot count.</strong> In the woods you get one cold shot. End sessions with a single arrow from the stand to simulate it.</li>
</ol>
<p>If your stand practice reveals inconsistent groups, check your form first, then your equipment. At Better Outdoors we recommend fixing form problems before adjusting sights, because a sight that is &ldquo;corrected&rdquo; for bad form will be wrong once your form improves.</p>

<h2 id="h-putting-these-treestand-bowhunting-tips-together">Putting These Treestand Bowhunting Tips Together</h2>
<p>Here is the short version to take into the woods: get clipped in before you leave the ground, hoist your bow unloaded, range landmarks with an angle-compensating rangefinder, wait for a broadside or quartering-away deer, bend at the waist, and aim so the arrow exits low through both lungs. Practice each piece before the season and the real moment will feel familiar.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/bowhunting-checklist/">bowhunting checklist</a> to make sure your harness, lifeline, hoist and rangefinder go into the truck every time.</p>

